  "body": "## Change and mathematical scope\n\n**Scoped negative result; no new kappa.** This closes the exactly-two projected-product branch left open by Cinna Davis's PR #1373. For the fixed P112 pair **(4,6)**, arbitrary GL(3,C) mixing of two coordinate terms with one nonzero free rectangle retains sharp whole-decoder COPY sum **64** and cannot lower the separate COPY power bill for `0 < t <= 1` at unchanged width and normalization.\n\nThe new compensation argument gives `|U| <= 2^(r_comp+1)-1`. If the compensation rank is at most one, at most three rows can disappear, preserving both other ranks. Otherwise the ranks dominate `(7,7,2)`, whose concave power bill is at least that of `(8,8,0)`. The three-product argument belongs to PR #1373; both new implementations replay its finite premise before combining the branches.\n\nA literal failure control shows why all terms matter: omitting compensation falsely reports **63**, while the complete bill is **70**. Identity mixing attains 64, including a rank-nine masked completion.\n\nOnly `research/p112-two-product-compensation/` is added. The [written proof](https://github.com/bbMisk/integer-mult-bounds/blob/8d53667d9bb09d9b1ffb0f865b5c8dc230eb02f6/research/p112-two-product-compensation/PROOF.md) states all hypotheses. Other coordinate pairs, zero-factor families, more mixed coordinates, multiple rectangles, shared copies and changed normalization are outside scope. No physical supplier, full paid recurrence, new multiplication bound or formal multiplication theorem is claimed.\n\n## Task and overlap\n\n- OS seed **46**, structural decoder track; `complex-masked-decoder` card, scope refined to the open two-product branch. Three candidate tasks and the local claim are in TASK.json. No pinned sprint issue existed.\n- Exact base: `cd91ac8e2652bfee6bf09da3b8724253bcf78f5f`; contribution: `8d53667d9bb09d9b1ffb0f865b5c8dc230eb02f6`. Refreshed upstream `410026dc5513b87db92ae98571d1d5984c69a983` preserves every live source pin and the selected record.\n- PR #1373: `3eaef3e4427068b33847dc1147c8a2e5709a970e`, the credited three-product predecessor for this same pair. Its seed and pair are not claimed as novelty.\n- PR #1331: `f08b698cc2bd74e2425486728a008e531fc35f0e`, Arham Tahir's one-coordinate predecessor and survivor-containment method.\n- PR #1375: `4a533aff2a7a87c9b36e077a31774b332a7f64bd`, @commdata2338's all-pair small-rectangle result. This contribution removes the support-size cap for nonzero rectangles on (4,6).\n- Concurrent PR #1432: `4bcc49e3f4041018b0607da46a87d8928a6e0fd0`, Hanson Sun / bbMisk's fixed 24-row (0,1) result, including zero amplitudes. Its reported `(7,8,7)` pattern is independently corroborated here; the new contribution is the arbitrary-support compensation-rank argument. It is not a code or theorem dependency.\n- The final recorded overlap scan completely paginated **797 open PRs and 149 discussion records**, without warnings; nearest heads were rechecked before submission. Source SHA256/Git-blob pins and source-specific notices are retained.\n\n## Validation\n\nPython **3.12.13**, standard library only:\n\n```sh\npython3.12 -B scripts/research_package.py --package research/p112-two-product-compensation\n```\n\nExpected: `PASS_REGISTERED_RESEARCH_PACKAGE research/p112-two-product-compensation outcome=negative-result`.\n\nBoth exact engines agree on all five strata, **2,555** character evaluations, **2,800** one-sided ranks and **145,600** credited three-product records. They also check all eight baseline coordinate charges. The primary checker verifies eight Gaussian-rational fixtures (**100,352** entries) and eight semantic controls. The separate label-based checker verifies five sharp erasure boundaries and an affine omission control.\n\nThe registered replay, optimized Python **3.14.6** checks, three CLI corruption/export test groups, **20** repository workflow tests, selected-record integrity and whitespace checks pass. Receipt SHA256: `8ee4249397f879fecfac63695fe547badddd8492533279689c71079ae943aa1b`. VALIDATION.json binds the proof and executed checkers.\n\nThe independent implementation covers the finite premises; it does not separately replay the Gaussian-rational fixture implementation. Both implementations have the same AI-assisted author. Full selected-supplier cold replay, native admission, hosted CI and proof-assistant checking were not run locally. The compiler, realization, hosting, precision/tape, analytic and bit-interface obligations remain inherited.\n\n## Credit\n\nCredit Cinna Davis / PR1373; Arham Tahir / PR1331; @commdata2338 / PR1375; concurrent Hanson Sun / bbMisk / PR1432; Mihir Srivastava / PR845; DreamingOfClouds / PR717 and PR446; Joel Pulikkan / GamingPuzzled / PR620; and the wider lineage in the retained notices.\n\nPrepared for **Hanson Sun (@bbMisk)** with substantial **OpenAI Codex** assistance in mathematics, code, checking and prose. Apache-2.0; no independent human review is claimed.\n\n## Open next questions\n\nThe README and TASK.json identify a bounded extension to three or more mixed coordinate terms: derive the minimal-active-product normal form and compensation-support bound before synthesis. New projective support classes and simultaneous survivor-rank estimates are the blockers. No gain is predicted.\n",
